96
00:24:22,644 --> 00:24:34,743
As this is a two-dimensional figure, if I want to access some of this data of this object, I have to go through a method.

97
00:24:36,443 --> 00:24:42,443
Wherever I draw a line, it will be passing among a method.

98
00:24:43,804 --> 00:24:49,243
And maybe in this case, it's passing along one method.

99
00:24:49,748 --> 00:24:57,827
This means that the data of an object shouldn't be able to access directly.

100
00:24:58,788 --> 00:25:07,668
This is called, and also the methods and the data of an object are connected together as a whole.

101
00:25:08,016 --> 00:25:10,776
This is called encapsulation principle.

102
00:25:11,436 --> 00:25:15,235
The data and the methods of an object cannot be separated.

103
00:25:15,836 --> 00:25:19,876
And moreover, data is accessed through methods of an object.

104
00:25:20,876 --> 00:25:31,715
When we make some analogies between functions of procedural programming and methods of object-oriented languages,

105
00:25:34,259 --> 00:25:40,679
In procedural programming, data and functions are apart from each arc.

106
00:25:41,980 --> 00:25:48,559
In, for example, C program, you can build some struct types.

107
00:25:49,011 --> 00:25:55,351
Asign some values, called some fields into a structure.

108
00:25:55,831 --> 00:26:03,652
And this structure, as a whole, will be represented, will be representing the data of an entity.

109
00:26:04,612 --> 00:26:12,811
However, when you write functions, those are not associated with the struct that you have called.

110
00:26:13,683 --> 00:26:27,824
The best you do is to pass some struct elements as parameters or create some struct elements in functions.

111
00:26:28,384 --> 00:26:34,983
But the data and the functions do not belong to each other.

112
00:26:35,344 --> 00:26:40,344
They are somewhat related, but they are not treated as a whole.

113
00:26:42,099 --> 00:27:04,500
On the contrary, the data of an object and the methods of an object are connected, they are treated as a whole, and other types of objects can access the data of different objects according to the object orientation rules.

114
00:27:05,299 --> 00:27:14,139
You can limit accessing of data to the outside other objects as much as you wish.

